# NPS-HS-SPANGLER-HENRY: "Historical Statement, Henry Spangler Farm Buildings", Frederick Tilberg, Gettysburg National Military Park, August 31, 1956 (a photocopied typescript of 10 pages whose text layer is a damaged OCR).
# Every quote below is typed as the PAGE IMAGE reads (pages 2, 4, 5, 6 and 8 were looked at on 2026-11-16, digits read from the image; the figure 4,800 of page 2 was enlarged to be sure) and aligned by scripts/ocrq.py with the OCR text (scripts/ocr.py) into {{saved|fixed}} pairs; the compiled file is extracts/src/d0-henry-spangler.xdef.
# This is the HENRY Spangler farm on the Emmitsburg Road west of the Emmitsburg Road ridge, NOT the George Spangler farm on Hospital Road (the XI Corps hospital; repository id bld-spangler-farm, hospital H-06): no extract here carries those two ids.
@ NPS-HS-SPANGLER-HENRY
%images 2, 4, 5, 6, 8

2 | general | Henry Spangler farm: lies between the Emmitsburg Road (east) and Seminary Ridge (west); the Codori farm bounds it on the north and the Sherfy farm on the south (as bounded and owned in 1956) | - | ~1956 (boundaries and owners as of the statement; the road and the ridge are fixed features) | stated | The statement says the Henry Spangler farm lies between the Emmitsburg Road on the east and Seminary Ridge on the west, bounded on the north mainly by the Codori farm, then owned by the United States Government, and on the south by the Sherfy farm, then owned by the Smyser Folkenroth estate | @@The Henry Spangler farm lies between the Emmitsburg Road (on the east) and Seminary Ridge on the west. It is bounded on the north mainly, by the Codori farm, owned by the United States Government, and on the south by the Sherfy farm, owned by the Smyser Folkenroth estate.
2 | fields | Henry Spangler farm: 130.84 acres when the Government bought it on March 28, 1955 (typed 130;84 in the typescript, read as the decimal point) | - | ~1955 (the acreage of the farm at its purchase; the statement gives no 1863 acreage) | stated | The statement gives the farm, as the United States Government bought it on March 28, 1955, as 130.84 acres | @@The farm, consisting of 130.84 acres, was purchased by the United States Government March 28, 1955.
2 | woodlots | Spangler's Woods on Seminary Ridge: a 25-acre parcel that was part of the original Spangler farm until the Government bought it in 1899 | - | ~pre-1899 (part of the original Spangler farm until 1899; the statement gives no 1863 date) | stated | The statement says Spangler's Woods on Seminary Ridge, a 25-acre parcel that was part of the original Spangler farm, was purchased by the Government in 1899 | @@Spangler's Woods on Seminary Ridge, a 25-acre parcel which was part of the original Spangler farm, was purchased by the Government in 1899.
2 | general | Henry Spangler farm in the battle: Pickett's division formed battle lines and awaited orders to attack in the low ground in the vicinity of the Spangler buildings | - | 1863-07 (July 3 by the statement's own page 4) | stated | The statement says the entire farm is closely associated with Pickett's Charge because the division formed battle lines and awaited orders to attack in the low ground in the vicinity of the Spangler buildings | @@The entire farm is closely associated with Pickett's Charge, as a continuous line of artillery extended from south to north over this land and Pickett's entire division of 4,800 men formed battle lines and awaited orders to attack in the low ground in the vicinity of the Spangler buildings.
4 | general | Henry Spangler farm: on July 3 the low ground at the farm buildings was screened from the Union positions on Cemetery Ridge by the Emmitsburg Road ridge | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The statement says that, arriving late in the day at Spangler's Woods, the division moved forward near midday on July 3 to the low ground at the Spangler farm buildings, where its position was screened from the view of Union positions on Cemetery Ridge by the Emmitsburg Road ridge | @@Arriving late in the day at Spangler's Woods, the division moved forward near midday July 3, to the low ground at the Spangler farm buildings where their position was screened from the view of Union positions on Cemetery Ridge by the Emmitsburg Road ridge.
%basis Miss Ella M. Spangler, 88 years old in 1956, who lived in the house the greater part of her life, as the statement names her
4 | buildings | Henry Spangler house: a frame, brick and stone structure built about 1820 (Miss Ella M. Spangler's account) | STR-SPANGLER_H-HOUSE1 | 1820 (built about 1820, according to Miss Ella M. Spangler, aged 88 in 1956) | hedged | The statement says the Spangler house, a frame, brick and stone structure, was built about 1820, according to Miss Ella M. Spangler, 88 years old, who lived in the house the greater part of her life and until it was sold to the United States Government the year before | @@The Spangler house is a frame brick and stone structure built about 1820, according to Miss Ella M. Spangler, 88 years old, who lived in the house the greater part of her life and until it was sold to the U. S. Government last year.
%basis -
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler house: an extension (lean-to) on the west side tapered from the eaves of the main house to one story in height (the form before it was raised about 1880; its own date is not given) | STR-SPANGLER_H-HOUSE1 | ~pre-1880 (the form of the extension before it was raised about 1880; the statement does not say when the extension was built) | stated | The statement describes an extension on the west side of the house tapering from the eaves of the main house to one story in height, which was raised to two stories about 1880; it does not say when the extension was built | @@and an extension on the west side, tapered from the eaves of the main house to one-story in height. | @@About 1880 this lean-to was raised to a two-story height, the comb of the roof now being on a level with that of the original house.
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler house: about 1880 the lean-to was raised to two stories, its roof comb level with that of the original house | STR-SPANGLER_H-HOUSE1 | !about 1880 (the lean-to raised to two stories; after the battle) | stated | The statement says that about 1880 the lean-to was raised to a two-story height, the comb of its roof now level with that of the original house | @@About 1880 this lean-to was raised to a two-story height, the comb of the roof now being on a level with that of the original house.
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler house: the porch on the east side believed to have been added sometime after the battle | STR-SPANGLER_H-HOUSE1 | !after July 1863 (the porch on the east side, which the statement believes is not part of the original house) | hedged | The statement says that it believes the porch on the east side of the old house is not a part of the original house but was added sometime after the battle of Gettysburg | @@We believe that the porch on the east side of the old house is not a part of the original house but was added sometime after the battle of Gettysburg.
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler farm: the blacksmith shop 20 yards west of the house is reasonably certain to be of pre-war construction | - | pre-1863 (pre-war construction, hedged: reasonably certain) | hedged | The statement says it is reasonably certain that one outbuilding, the blacksmith shop located 20 yards west of the house, is of pre-war construction | @@It is reasonably certain that one outbuilding, the blacksmith shop located 20 yards west of the house, is of pre-war construction.
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler farm: a large frame summer kitchen and a small brick smokehouse built about 1890 (the page 8 caption calls the smokehouse probably early post-war) | - | !about 1890 (after the battle; the smokehouse is dated differently on page 8 of the same statement) | conflict | The statement says a large frame summer kitchen adjoining the house and a small brick smokehouse 30 yards to the northwest were built about 1890; its photograph caption on page 8 says the smokehouse is probably of early post-war construction and 30 feet beyond the shop | @@A large frame summer kitchen adjoining the house, and a small brick smokehouse 30 yards to the northwest were built about 1890.
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler farm: a long structure, used partly as a tool shed and garage and partly as a chicken house, built about 1890 | - | !about 1890 (after the battle) | stated | The statement says a long structure used partly as a tool shed and garage and partly as a chicken house was also built at about the same date, about 1890 | @@A long structure, used partly as a tool shed and garage, and partly as a chicken house, was also built at about the same date. | @@A large frame summer kitchen adjoining the house, and a small brick smokehouse 30 yards to the northwest were built about 1890.
%basis Miss Ella M. Spangler, 88 years old in 1956, for the barn built after the battle and its destruction, as the statement names her
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler barn: the original barn burned during the second day's battle | STR-SPANGLER_H-BARN1 | 1863-07-02 (the second day's battle) | stated | The statement says the original barn was burned during the second day's battle | @@The original barn was burned during the second day's battle.
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler barn: the barn built soon after the battle on the same foundations (Miss Spangler) was destroyed in 1932 when the present barn was built on the original foundations | STR-SPANGLER_H-BARN1 | ~1932 (the present barn stands on the original foundations according to the statement; it gives no dimensions of the 1863 barn) | hedged | The statement says that a barn built soon after the battle on the same foundations, according to Miss Spangler, was destroyed in 1932, when the present barn was likewise built upon the original foundations | @@A barn built soon thereafter on the same foundations, according to Miss Spangler, was destroyed in 1932, when the present barn was likewise built upon the original foundations.
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler barn: a barn built soon after the battle (destroyed in 1932) replaced the barn burned in the battle | STR-SPANGLER_H-BARN1 | !soon after July 1863 (the replacement barn, destroyed in 1932) | hedged | The statement says that a barn built soon after the battle on the same foundations, according to Miss Spangler, was destroyed in 1932 | @@A barn built soon thereafter on the same foundations, according to Miss Spangler, was destroyed in 1932, when the present barn was likewise built upon the original foundations.
%basis -
5 | buildings | Henry Spangler house: the plan of renovation of 1956 (foundation repointed, front porch repaired, second floor addition partitions relocated, interior and exterior repainted) | STR-SPANGLER_H-HOUSE1 | ~1956 (a plan of renovation and repair; the house as renovated is not an 1863 state) | stated | The statement says the plan of renovation and repair of the house does not intend to alter the exterior appearance and lists raking and repointing the foundation, repairing the front porch, relocating partitions in the second floor addition and repainting the interior and exterior of the house and summer kitchen | @@In the present plan of renovation and repair of the house, it is not intended to alter the exterior appearance. It is planned to rake and repoint the foundation, repair the front porch, replace water piping and electric wiring, install oil fired boiler, relocate partitions in second floor addition, clean and repaint interior and exterior of the house and summer kitchen, and install kitchen cabinet and sink.
5 | fields | Henry Spangler farm: in 1956 the tenant farmed under a special use permit that required farm conditions as near as possible to those at the time of the battle | - | ~1956 (a lease condition; the fields as farmed after 1955 are not an 1863 state) | stated | The statement says the tenant of the Spangler farm land operates under a special use permit that requires him to maintain farm conditions as nearly as possible to the condition at the time of the battle | @@The tenant of the Spangler farm land operates under a special use permit which requires him to maintain farm conditions as nearly as possible to the condition at the time of the battle.
6 | buildings | Henry Spangler house: the caption calls the original part a two-story frame house and the lean-to mainly of stone, later raised to two stories with the second story of brick | STR-SPANGLER_H-HOUSE1 | ~pre-1880 (photograph caption of 1956; the lean-to of stone before it was raised, its brick second story after the raising about 1880) | stated | The caption says the north end of the original two-story frame house appears at the left and the lean-to, mainly of stone construction and later raised to two stories with the second story of brick, at the right; page 5 dates the raising of the lean-to about 1880 | @@The north end of the original two-story frame house appears at the left, and the lean-to (mainly of stone construction and later raised to two stories, the second story of brick), appears at the right. | @5: @@About 1880 this lean-to was raised to a two-story height, the comb of the roof now being on a level with that of the original house.
6 | buildings | Henry Spangler farm: the shed at the extreme right of the photograph is a post-war construction | - | !post-war (a shed beside the house, undated otherwise) | stated | The caption says the shed at the extreme right is a post-war construction | @@The shed at the extreme right is a post-war construction.
8 | buildings | Henry Spangler farm: the blacksmith shop has a lean-to, possibly the forge housing, at its near end (photograph caption) | - | ~1956 (photograph caption; the shop itself is pre-war by page 5, the lean-to is not dated) | hedged | The caption says the Spangler blacksmith shop has a lean-to, possibly the forge housing, at the near end of the shop | @@The Spangler blacksmith shop, with a lean-to, possibly the forge housing, at the near end of the shop.
8 | buildings | Henry Spangler farm: a small, square brick smokehouse probably of early post-war construction, 30 feet beyond the blacksmith shop (the text on page 5 says about 1890 and 30 yards northwest of the house) | - | !early post-war (probably; the same statement's page 5 gives about 1890) | conflict | The caption says a small, square brick smokehouse, probably of early post-war construction, is located 30 feet beyond the shop, while the statement's text on page 5 says a small brick smokehouse 30 yards to the northwest of the house was built about 1890 | @@A small, square brick smokehouse, probably of early post-war construction, is located 30 feet beyond the shop.
